    "case_id": "21",
    "case_summary": "An obese man in his mid-30s with no prior systemic or ocular disorders presented to a local emergency department after waking with vision loss in his right eye. The night prior, he had been drinking alcohol heavily and took 2 buprenorphine hydrochloride/naloxone hydrochloride pills. He denied any trauma. He also experienced pain, numbness, and erythematous induration of his right eyelids and forearm that increased throughout the day.\n\nVisual acuity: No light perception in right eye (OD) and 20/20 in left eye (OS). Right eye showed tense eyelid edema, chemosis, corneal haze, and restricted motility in all fields of gaze. Right pupil was mid-dilated with a relative afferent pupillary defect. Intraocular pressure was 37 mm Hg OD and 21 mm Hg OS. Ophthalmoscopic examination revealed tortuous vessels, serous retinal detachment sparing the inferior periphery, and macular whitening. Left eye examination was normal.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\nCreatinine level: 2.2 mg/dL\nCreatine kinase level: 29,320 U/L\n\n- Imaging Studies:\n1. Noncontrast computed tomography of head and orbits: Showed periorbital soft-tissue swelling\n\n2. Optical Coherence Tomography:\nImage Title: OCT of the Right Eye\nImage Description: Demonstrates severe disorganization of the retinal architecture, subretinal fluid, and sub-retinal pigment epithelial fluid\n\n3. Fluorescein Angiography:\nImage Title: Fluorescein Angiography of the Right Eye\nImage Description: Shows delayed choroidal filling, delayed retinal arterial filling, patchy temporal hyperfluorescence, diffuse leakage and poor vascular perfusion in various phases\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Ocular Compartment Syndrome\nB. Orbital Compartment Syndrome\nC. Saturday night retinopathy with orbital compression syndrome\nD. Orbital compartment syndrome secondary to rhabdomyolysis\n",
